WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.175
The Model Context Protocol standardizes how applications connect to tools and other

00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:06.038
context providers.

00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:10.459
It defines a transport and message contract; it does not make a

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:14.730
server trustworthy or grant a user permission to access all advertised capabilities.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.376
Authenticate the connection, authorize each operation, and review the actual tool behavior.

00:00:20.526 --> 00:00:25.754
Multi-agent designs divide work into roles such as retrieval, drafting, and review.

00:00:25.904 --> 00:00:31.755
Their messages need explicit schemas, source provenance, deadlines, and a single authority

00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:33.217
for state-changing actions.

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:36.479
Correlated model errors can survive several reviewers.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:40.404
Begin with a single bounded workflow and add agents only when measurement

00:00:40.404 --> 00:00:41.347
shows a benefit.

00:00:41.497 --> 00:00:46.009
A research worker can propose policy evidence, a support worker can draft

00:00:46.009 --> 00:00:49.018
an answer, and a verifier can check citations.

00:00:49.168 --> 00:00:50.661
None should approve a refund.

00:00:50.811 --> 00:00:55.328
A coordinator merges results under one task ID and rejects evidence outside

00:00:55.328 --> 00:00:56.833
the customer’s access scope.

00:00:56.983 --> 00:01:02.529
The supplied capstone uses one agent; MCP servers and cooperating agents are

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:03.915
advanced extension projects.

00:01:04.065 --> 00:01:07.906
Write an MCP tool contract for read-only order lookup and a threat

00:01:07.906 --> 00:01:09.506
model for an untrusted server.

00:01:09.656 --> 00:01:14.014
Then compare a single-agent answer with a three-stage workflow on the same

00:01:14.014 --> 00:01:14.740
evaluation cases.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.960
Record extra model calls, latency, task success, and failure modes.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.635
Keep credentials out of prompts and distinguish transport authentication from business authorization.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:30.204
If the multi-agent version is slower without a measurable quality gain, retain

00:01:30.204 --> 00:01:31.308
the simpler workflow.

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:32.130
Question.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.457
Does MCP solve authorization?

00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:35.183
Answer.

00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:35.817
No.

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:41.247
It standardizes interaction; the application and server must enforce access policy.

00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:42.068
Question.

00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:44.856
What prevents agents from endlessly delegating?

00:01:45.006 --> 00:01:45.583
Answer.

00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:51.903
A coordinator-owned task graph, bounded depth and calls, explicit completion criteria, and

00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:52.417
deadlines.
